% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:tv-intro}

Compiler optimizations are among the most complex and error-prone components of
modern software systems.  An optimizing compiler transforms a source program
through a long pipeline of passes, each of which must preserve the program's
semantics.  When an optimization pass contains a bug, it can silently introduce
incorrect behavior---generating code that computes the wrong result without any
warning or error message.  Such bugs are particularly insidious because they
violate the programmer's trust in the compiler.

\emph{Translation validation}~\cite{pnueli1998translation,pnueli2001translation}
is a technique for verifying that each compiler transformation preserves the
program's semantics.  Rather than verifying the compiler itself (which is the
approach of compiler verification, \cref{ch:compiler-verification}), translation
validation verifies each \emph{individual compilation}: after the compiler runs,
a validator checks that the output program is semantically equivalent to the
input program.

Translation validation was pioneered by Pnueli, Siegel, and
Singerman~\cite{pnueli1998translation} in 1998 and has since been applied to
verify optimizations in production compilers, including
LLVM~\cite{lopes2015alive2,bhargava2020towards}.  The technique provides a
practical middle ground between full compiler verification (which is extremely
expensive) and no verification at all.

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of translation validation.
We begin with the fundamental concepts (\cref{sec:tv-concepts}), examine
equivalence checking techniques (\cref{sec:tv-equivalence}), discuss SMT-based
approaches (\cref{sec:tv-smt}), describe the Alive2 system for LLVM
(\cref{sec:tv-alive2}), and conclude with integration into optimization
pipelines (\cref{sec:tv-integration}).

% ============================================================
% What is Translation Validation?
% ============================================================
\section{What is Translation Validation?}\label{sec:tv-concepts}

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:tv-problem}

\begin{definition}[Translation Validation]\label{def:tv}
\emph{Translation validation} is the problem of verifying, for a specific
compilation run, that the output program $P'$ is semantically equivalent to the
input program $P$.  Formally, given $P$ and $P'$, decide whether:
\[
\forall \sigma.\; \SemBrack{P}(\sigma) = \SemBrack{P'}(\sigma)
\]
where $\SemBrack{\cdot}$ denotes the denotational semantics of the program and
$\sigma$ ranges over all possible initial states.
\end{definition}

Translation validation differs from compiler verification in a crucial way:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Compiler verification} proves that the compiler is correct for
      \emph{all} inputs (a universal property).
\item \textbf{Translation validation} proves that a \emph{specific} compilation
      is correct (an instance property).
\end{itemize}

This makes translation validation more practical: it does not require a formal
model of the entire compiler, only a formal model of the source and target
languages.

\subsection{Validation vs.\ Verification}\label{sec:tv-vs-verify}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Translation Validation vs.\ Compiler Verification}\label{tab:tv-vs-verify}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Translation Validation} & \textbf{Compiler Verification} \\
\hline
Scope & Per-compilation & Universal (all compilations) \\
Cost & Proportional to program size & Proportional to compiler size \\
Trust base & Source/target semantics & Compiler implementation \\
False positives & Possible (incomplete validator) & None (if sound) \\
Applicability & Any compiler & Requires formal compiler model \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Soundness and Completeness}\label{sec:tv-soundness}

\begin{definition}[Soundness and Completeness of Validators]\label{def:tv-sound}
A translation validator is:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sound:} If the validator accepts, then $P$ and $P'$ are truly
      equivalent.  (No false positives.)
\item \textbf{Complete:} If $P$ and $P'$ are truly equivalent, then the
      validator accepts.  (No false negatives.)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

In practice, validators are typically sound but incomplete: they may reject some
valid translations (false negatives) but never accept an invalid one.

% ============================================================
% Equivalence Checking
% ============================================================
\section{Equivalence Checking}\label{sec:tv-equivalence}

\subsection{Semantic Equivalence}\label{sec:tv-semantic}

Two programs are \emph{semantically equivalent} if they produce the same
observable behavior for all inputs.

\begin{definition}[Semantic Equivalence]\label{def:semantic-equiv}
Programs $P$ and $P'$ are \emph{semantically equivalent}, written $P \equiv P'$,
if for all initial states $\sigma$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $P$ terminates on $\sigma$ iff $P'$ terminates on $\sigma$.
\item If $P$ terminates on $\sigma$ with final state $\sigma'$, then $P'$
      terminates on $\sigma$ with the same final state $\sigma'$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

For programs with side effects (I/O, system calls), equivalence must also
account for the observable effects.

\subsection{Structural Equivalence}\label{sec:tv-structural}

In some cases, equivalence can be established by \emph{structural comparison}:
showing that the two programs have the same structure after accounting for
renaming, reordering, and other syntactic transformations.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Alpha-equivalence:} Programs are equivalent up to variable renaming.
\item \textbf{Commutativity:} Independent operations can be reordered.
\item \textbf{Algebraic identities:} $x + 0 = x$, $x \times 1 = x$, etc.
\end{itemize}

Structural equivalence is efficient to check but incomplete: many semantically
equivalent programs are not structurally equivalent.

% ============================================================
% Bounded Equivalence
% ============================================================
\section{Bounded Equivalence}\label{sec:tv-bounded}

\subsection{Bounded Translation Validation}\label{sec:tv-bounded-def}

\emph{Bounded translation validation} checks equivalence only for inputs of
bounded size or for executions of bounded length.

\begin{definition}[Bounded Equivalence]\label{def:bounded-equiv}
Programs $P$ and $P'$ are \emph{$k$-equivalent} if for all initial states
$\sigma$ with $|\sigma| \leq k$:
\[
\SemBrack{P}(\sigma) = \SemBrack{P'}(\sigma)
\]
where $|\sigma|$ is some measure of the size of the state (e.g., the magnitude
of integer values, the length of arrays).
\end{definition}

Bounded equivalence is decidable (for finite bounds) and can be checked by
enumerating inputs or by symbolic execution with bounded path length.

\subsection{Unrolling-Based Validation}\label{sec:tv-unrolling}

For programs with loops, bounded equivalence can be checked by unrolling loops
up to a fixed bound and checking the resulting loop-free programs for
equivalence.  This is the basis of bounded model checking applied to translation
validation.

