% SMT-Based Approaches
% ============================================================
\section{SMT-Based Approaches}\label{sec:tv-smt}

\subsection{Encoding Equivalence as SMT}\label{sec:tv-smt-encoding}

The most powerful approach to translation validation uses SMT solvers to check
equivalence.  The idea is to encode the negation of equivalence as an SMT
formula and check its satisfiability:

\[
\phi_{\mathit{diff}} = \exists \sigma.\; \SemBrack{P}(\sigma) \neq \SemBrack{P'}(\sigma)
\]

If $\phi_{\mathit{diff}}$ is unsatisfiable, the programs are equivalent.  If it
is satisfiable, the satisfying assignment provides a counterexample input.

\subsection{Lockstep Simulation}\label{sec:tv-lockstep}

\emph{Lockstep simulation} encodes both programs in a common SMT formula,
executing them in lockstep and asserting that their outputs differ at some point.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{SMT-Based Translation Validation}\label{alg:tv-smt}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Source program $P$, target program $P'$
\Ensure ``Valid'' or counterexample
\State Encode $P$ as SMT formula $\phi_P$ (using symbolic inputs)
\State Encode $P'$ as SMT formula $\phi_{P'}$ (using same symbolic inputs)
\State $\phi_{\mathit{diff}} \gets \phi_P \land \phi_{P'} \land (\mathit{output}_P \neq \mathit{output}_{P'})$
\If{$\mathrm{SMT.Solve}(\phi_{\mathit{diff}}) = \mathit{unsat}$}
  \State \Return ``Valid''
\Else
  \State $\sigma \gets \mathrm{SMT.Solve}(\phi_{\mathit{diff}})$
  \State \Return ``Invalid: counterexample $\sigma$''
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Handling Non-Determinism}\label{sec:tv-nondet}

When programs contain non-determinism (e.g., from undefined behavior in C),
equivalence must account for the non-deterministic choices.  Two approaches:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Refinement:} $P'$ refines $P$ if every behavior of $P'$ is an
      allowed behavior of $P$.  This is appropriate when $P$ has undefined
      behavior that $P'$ resolves in a specific way.
\item \textbf{Equivalence of non-deterministic programs:} $P$ and $P'$ are
      equivalent if they have the same set of allowed behaviors.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Memory Equivalence}\label{sec:tv-memory}

Equivalence checking for programs with memory requires reasoning about the
contents of memory at each program point.  The standard approach is to use the
theory of arrays to model memory:

\begin{itemize}
\item Each memory state is an array mapping addresses to values.
\item Load and store operations are modeled using $\mathit{read}$ and
      $\mathit{write}$ on arrays.
\item Equivalence requires that corresponding memory locations contain the same
      values at corresponding program points.
\end{itemize}

