\section{Alive2 for LLVM}\label{sec:tv-alive2}

\subsection{Background: Alive}\label{sec:tv-alive}

Alive~\cite{lopes2015alive} is a domain-specific language for specifying LLVM
optimizations, together with a verifier that checks each optimization for
correctness.  Each Alive optimization is specified as a pair of LLVM IR
fragments (source and target), and the verifier checks that the target refines
the source for all possible inputs.

Alive identified several bugs in LLVM's optimization passes and in the official
documentation of LLVM's instructions.  However, Alive operates on \emph{hand-written}
optimization specifications; it does not validate the optimizations actually
performed by the LLVM compiler.

\subsection{Alive2}\label{sec:tv-alive2-system}

Alive2~\cite{lopes2015alive2} extends Alive to perform \emph{automated}
translation validation of LLVM IR transformations.  It intercepts the
optimization pipeline, captures the IR before and after each pass, and checks
equivalence using an SMT-based validator.

