\subsection{Key Challenges in Alive2}\label{sec:tv-alive2-challenges}

Alive2 must handle several challenging aspects of LLVM IR:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Undefined behavior (UB):} LLVM IR has many forms of UB (signed
      overflow, null dereference, use-after-free, etc.).  An optimization that
      removes UB is valid only if the original program was already guaranteed
      to be free of that UB.
\item \textbf{Poison values:} LLVM distinguishes \emph{undef} (any value) and
      \emph{poison} (a value that makes any use of it UB).  Correct reasoning
      about poison is essential for validating optimizations involving vector
      operations and bit manipulation.
\item \textbf{Memory model:} LLVM's memory model includes allocation,
      deallocation, and aliasing rules that must be faithfully modeled.
\item \textbf{Scalability:} The validator must handle large IR functions
      efficiently, requiring careful encoding strategies.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Results}\label{sec:tv-alive2-results}

Alive2 has discovered dozens of bugs in LLVM's optimization passes, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item Incorrect handling of undefined behavior in dead code elimination.
\item Incorrect constant folding for floating-point operations.
\item Incorrect handling of \texttt{poison} in vector optimizations.
\item Incorrect loop unrolling that changed the semantics of programs with
      signed integer overflow.
\end{itemize}

