% Integration with Optimization Pipelines
% ============================================================
\section{Integration with Optimization Pipelines}\label{sec:tv-integration}

\subsection{Where to Validate}\label{sec:tv-where}

Translation validation can be applied at different points in the compilation
pipeline:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{After each pass:} Validates each individual transformation.
      Provides fine-grained error localization but increases overhead.
\item \textbf{After groups of passes:} Validates batches of transformations.
      Reduces overhead but makes error localization harder.
\item \textbf{At the end of compilation:} Validates the entire compilation.
      Lowest overhead but hardest to debug when validation fails.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Performance Overhead}\label{sec:tv-overhead}

The performance overhead of translation validation depends on:
\begin{itemize}
\item The size of the programs being validated.
\item The complexity of the equivalence checking algorithm.
\item Whether validation is done online (during compilation) or offline (after
      compilation, as a separate step).
\end{itemize}

For Alive2, the overhead is typically 2--10x the compilation time, depending on
the optimization pass and the size of the function being optimized.  This is
acceptable for debugging and testing but too expensive for production use.

\subsection{Selective Validation}\label{sec:tv-selective}

To reduce overhead, selective validation validates only the most error-prone
passes or only the transformations that are most likely to be incorrect.
Heuristics for selecting which transformations to validate include:
\begin{itemize}
\item Validate passes that modify control flow (most error-prone).
\item Validate passes that reason about undefined behavior.
\item Skip validation for simple, well-tested passes (e.g., constant folding
      of integer arithmetic).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Continuous Validation}\label{sec:tv-continuous}

Translation validation can be integrated into the compiler's test suite and
continuous integration pipeline.  Every change to the compiler is tested by
compiling a large corpus of programs with validation enabled, catching
regressions before they reach users.

\begin{insight}
Translation validation embodies a profound shift in how we think about compiler
correctness.  Rather than proving the compiler correct once and for all, we
verify each compilation individually.  This is analogous to the shift from
static typing (proving all programs correct at compile time) to dynamic checking
(verifying each execution at run time).  The advantage is practicality: we can
validate any compiler, even one that we cannot formally model.  The disadvantage
is that we can never be sure we have found all bugs---only that the programs we
have compiled so far are correct.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
Translation validation was introduced by Pnueli, Siegel, and
Singerman~\cite{pnueli1998translation} in 1998, in the context of verifying
reactive systems.  The idea was adapted to compiler optimization by
Necula~\cite{necula2000translation} in 2000, who proposed validating
optimizations in the Proof-Carrying Code framework.  The first practical
translation validator for a production compiler was the work of
Bhargava et al.~\cite{bhargava2020towards} on LLVM, building on the Alive
framework~\cite{lopes2015alive}.  Alive2~\cite{lopes2015alive2} has become the
standard tool for validating LLVM optimizations and has discovered dozens of
real-world compiler bugs.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:tv-summary}

Translation validation provides a practical approach to ensuring compiler
correctness.  By verifying each individual compilation rather than the compiler
as a whole, it sidesteps the enormous difficulty of formal compiler verification
while still providing strong guarantees about the correctness of compiled code.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Scaling to whole-program validation:} Current tools validate
      individual functions; extending to whole programs requires handling
      inter-procedural optimizations.
\item \textbf{Handling complex language semantics:} Languages like C++ have
      extremely complex semantics that are difficult to model precisely.
\item \textbf{Reducing overhead:} Making translation validation fast enough for
      production use remains an open challenge.
\item \textbf{Integration with compiler development:} Making translation
      validation a standard part of the compiler development workflow.
\end{itemize}
